  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 207 PM MDT TUE OCT 27 2026\nFriday, a closed low off the Pacific Northwest Coast amplifies \nalong a southward trough axis capturing a pocket of tropical \nmoisture. As a deep Alaskan Low strengthens, it captures the \nclosed low, trough, and tropical moisture. As they move inland, \nthe low heads northeastward and carries the moisture and colder \nair inside the trough up through the forecast area. The entire \nsynoptic process takes place from Friday through early Monday, \nduring which precipitation chances increase and \ntemperatures/snow levels fall. The primary cold front passes \nearly Sunday morning in the latest ensemble means, so the \nweather Friday and Saturday feature modest precip chances \n(10-40%, higher over terrain) and snow levels around 5-7 kft \nMSL. Temperatures during this period hover around to just above \nnormal for this time of year. Halloween evening (Saturday), \nprecipitation chances increase sharply to 40-70% as temperatures\nand snow levels begin to dip. Sunday morning snow levels drop \nto 4-6 kft MSL, and by Monday they drop further to 3.5-4.5 kft \nMSL. At least some snowfall is likely above 5 kft MSL, and at \nleast some chance of snow exists down to 4 kft MSL even though \nprecipitation chances begin to decrease Sunday night, limiting \nhow much precipitation falls when snow levels are lowest. Dry \nconditions will prevail through the beginning of next week. \nSeasonable or below normal temperatures will continue through \nthe long-term period.",
